+To measure the benefit of a constraint, we compare each constrained
+run with its matched baseline: the same dataset, model, fold and seed,
+within the same experimental batch. The paired gain is simply the
+constrained run's Dice minus the baseline's Dice. Scores are reported
+as percentages and gains as percentage points: for example, an increase
+from 75\% to 78\% is a gain of 3 percentage points.
+
+We average these paired gains across the three seeds within each fold
+and then equally across folds. Their sample standard deviation describes
+variation between the three fold-mean gains. Positive-change counts
+state how many of the nine matched runs improve on their baseline.
+These standard deviations describe the variation observed in this
+study; they are not confidence intervals over independent datasets.
+Seeds share data, training pools overlap and configuration selection
+is exploratory. Any variation across individual volumes is reported
+separately.
 
